1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"show-the-comparison","number":"01","title":"Make the comparison visible.","mode":"Table search","scale":"slide","dek":"When presenting several numbers, decide which comparison the audience should make. Arrange or plot the values so that comparison is easy to see.","before":[{"title":"The full table","kind":"image","image":"/originals/china-shock-p20.png","alt":"Original table with 22 commuting zones and four numeric columns. North Hickory and Raleigh-Cary are highlighted.","label":"REGIONAL COMPARISON · 2000","id":"regional-table","sourcePage":"20"}],"after":[{"title":"Two exposed regions had different workforces.","kind":"regions","alt":"Year 2000: North Hickory, NC has manufacturing employment share 43.0%, BA share of ages 18–64 15.6%, population 377.5 thousand. Raleigh-Cary, NC: 17.0%, 34.2%, and 1,420.0 thousand. Import-penetration change, 2000–2012: 4.40 and 3.42 percentage points, respectively.","revises":["regional-table"],"subtitle":"Workforce composition in 2000"}],"diagnosis":"The original already tells us which two rows matter. A revision can carry that emphasis through to the comparison itself.","changes":["Keep the two highlighted places together.","Use a common scale for each pair of percentages.","Keep population and exposure in a separate context line."],"preserved":"All eight numbers for the highlighted regions, with their dates and units.","deferred":"The other 20 regions remain in the original and in the complete data download below.","practice":"Write down the comparison you intend to say aloud. Make that comparison the figure; keep the full table available.","principle":"Minimize tables with lots of numbers!","sourceType":"public","context":"The source highlights these same two regions; the revision develops that comparison.","beforeCaption":"","afterCaption":"","source":{"authors":"David Autor, David Dorn, Gordon Hanson","title":"On the Persistence of the China Shock","date":"2021-09-09","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/6a_ADH-BPEA-Slides-Final.pdf","pages":"20"},"values":["North Hickory: manufacturing share 43.0%; BA share of population aged 18–64 15.6%; population 377.5 thousand; import-penetration change 4.40 percentage points.","Raleigh-Cary: manufacturing share 17.0%; BA share 34.2%; population 1,420.0 thousand; import-penetration change 3.42 percentage points.","Shares and population: 2000. Import-penetration change: 2000–2012."],"topic":"International trade","point":"A table can contain all the relevant evidence while making the audience search for the relationship you want to discuss. Organize the display around that relationship: put comparable quantities together, label their units, and use a common scale where appropriate. Keep the full table available for other comparisons."}}]
